<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharnaces I of Pontus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 160 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharnaces I was king of Pontus from about 185 to about 160 BCE. He captured the Greek city of Sinope in 183 BCE and made it his capital, and fought a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and other Anatolian rulers that ended with the peace of 179 BCE, in which he gave up most of his gains in Galatia and Paphlagonia. He was the grandfather of Mithridates VI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus.jpg" alt="Pharnaces I of Pontus" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinastia_attalide_di_pergamo,_pharnakes_I,_tetradracma_del_ponto,_185-170_ac_ca.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
